{%- comment -%}
  AJ Brand shipping — the one true thing we can say about shipping this brand.

  WHY THIS IS DATA AND NOT COPY. Shipping on Collective is charged per shipment
  and every supplier prices it differently. Sticker Fire is $5.35 whether the
  cart holds one sticker or four; Dr. Watson's goes free above $44;
  prodigalpottery gets MORE expensive as you add items. A single hand-written
  line would be wrong for most of the catalogue and would go stale the week a
  supplier changes their rate card.

  So the shipping agent measures each supplier weekly and writes a
  `vendor_shipping` metaobject, and this snippet renders whatever that says.
  Nobody edits copy when a supplier changes; the agent notices.

  IT STAYS SILENT MORE OFTEN THAN IT SPEAKS, ON PURPOSE. Nine suppliers get more
  expensive per item — inviting a bigger basket there costs us money. Six have a
  free-shipping threshold their own catalogue cannot reach: August Uncommon Tea
  is free above $56 and sells one $14 product, so "add $42 more" is a promise
  with nothing behind it. Both cases render nothing at all.

  ── EMPHASIS IS A FUNCTION OF WHAT THE LINE IS DOING ──────────────────────────

  1 August. Three tones, and which one applies is decided here rather than by
  whoever places the block:

    FREE   — a pill. Free shipping is the strongest thing this store can say
             after the discount itself, and it was rendering at 0.85 opacity
             with a tick: whispered. The accent is a tint rather than a solid
             block so it does not compete with the saving pill directly above
             it; two solid pills stacked leaves the eye no way to rank them.
    PLAIN  — a normal line of body text. It NAMES A COST, so it is stated
             clearly and not sold.
    NUDGE  — "add this much more". Was the loudest thing in the block at
             weight 600, which had it shouting the ask and whispering the gift.
             Now plain, and CART-ONLY (see below).

  WHY THE NUDGE IS CART-ONLY NOW. It needs `cart_vendor_total` to mean anything,
  and no caller has ever passed one — so on the PDP `spent` was 0 and every
  threshold supplier printed "Add $44.00 more from this brand for free shipping"
  in bold, directly under the price, to a shopper with an empty cart who has not
  yet added the item in front of them. A demand at the point of highest
  purchase intent, out-weighting the price. On a page with no cart context the
  same fact is stated as a fact — "free over $44" — which motivates without
  asking for anything.

  ── ON PRINTING A SHIPPING NUMBER ─────────────────────────────────────────────

  `ship.cost` is a real quote from `draftOrderCalculate`, so it is what a
  customer would be charged and not what the supplier charges us. Three things
  keep it from being an exact promise: it is measured to ONE destination
  (Austin, TX), at QUANTITY 1, taking the CHEAPEST available rate. Hence "from",
  which is honest in all three directions and errs toward overstating the cost —
  the safe direction for a claim, and the same reasoning that keeps a delivery
  date off `aj-ships-from`.

  Saying it at all beats the alternative: late-discovered shipping is the
  single biggest cause of cart abandonment, and this store adds a parcel per
  supplier. Better refused on the PDP than abandoned at checkout.

  @param product - the product whose vendor we are describing
  @param cart_vendor_total - optional. Cart subtotal for THIS vendor, so the cart
         can say how far away free shipping is. Shipping is charged per supplier,
         so a whole-cart total would be the wrong number. ABSENT means "no cart
         context", which is a different thing from zero and is treated as such.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  # The product's OWN measured cost wins over the vendor's shape. Vendor shape
  # is a good predictor and not a guarantee: Fuse Audio ships six of seven
  # products free and charges $12 on a heavy radio. A badge is a promise, so it
  # reads the number measured for this exact product where one exists.
  assign own_cost = product.metafields.ship.cost.value
  assign checked_at = product.metafields.ship.checked_at.value
  assign vs = nil
  for entry in shop.metaobjects.vendor_shipping.values
    if entry.vendor.value == product.vendor
      assign vs = entry
      break
    endif
  endfor
-%}

{%- liquid
  assign shape = vs.shape.value
  assign msg = nil
  assign tone = 'plain'

  # A measured cost for THIS product overrules the vendor's shape, in both
  # directions. Zero is the strongest claim available. Non-zero MUTES the
  # brand-level "free" and "ships free" claims, which is the whole reason the
  # per-product number exists — Fuse Audio ships six of seven free and charges
  # $12 on a heavy radio, and the vendor shape would have promised free on it.
  assign measured = false
  if own_cost != blank
    assign measured = true
    assign cost_money = own_cost | times: 100 | round | money
  endif

  # ASKED AND GOT NOTHING IS NOT THE SAME AS NEVER ASKED. `checked_at` with no
  # `cost` means Shopify offered no rate at all for this product, which means it
  # cannot be shipped and therefore cannot be bought. Without this guard a
  # `free`-shaped vendor would have had its brand-level claim printed over the
  # top of an unshippable product — the most expensive sentence on the page.
  assign no_rate = false
  if measured == false and checked_at != blank
    assign no_rate = true
  endif

  # Absent is not zero. An empty cart and no cart at all want different copy.
  assign has_cart_context = false
  if cart_vendor_total != blank
    assign has_cart_context = true
    assign spent = cart_vendor_total | plus: 0
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  Gated on `no_rate` alone rather than on having a vendor entry. A per-product
  measured cost stands on its own — it needs no vendor shape to be true — so a
  supplier the weekly sweep has not reached yet can still say what shipping
  costs. With no vendor entry AND no measurement the branches below all fall
  through and nothing renders, which is the same silence as before.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- unless no_rate -%}
  {%- liquid
    if measured and own_cost == 0
      assign msg = 'Free shipping.'
      assign tone = 'free'

    elsif shape == 'free' and measured == false
      assign msg = 'Free shipping on this brand.'
      assign tone = 'free'

    elsif shape == 'flat' and measured == false
      assign msg = 'Anything else from this brand ships together, free.'
      assign tone = 'free'

    elsif shape == 'flat' and measured
      # Measured non-zero, but a flat supplier genuinely does carry extra items
      # for nothing. Both halves are useful and neither is free, so the line
      # states the cost first and the benefit second, plainly.
      assign msg = 'Shipping from ' | append: cost_money | append: '. Anything else from this brand ships with it, free.'

    elsif shape == 'threshold' and vs.reachable.value
      assign threshold = vs.free_threshold.value | plus: 0
      # Rounded explicitly: free_threshold is a decimal metafield and Liquid
      # arithmetic on those goes float, so `43.99 | times: 100` can land on
      # 4398.9999 and render as a price nobody would ever write down.
      assign threshold_money = threshold | times: 100 | round | money

      if has_cart_context
        if spent >= threshold
          assign msg = 'Shipping on this brand is free.'
          assign tone = 'free'
        else
          assign gap = threshold | minus: spent
          assign gap_money = gap | times: 100 | round | money
          assign msg = 'Add ' | append: gap_money | append: ' more from this brand for free shipping.'
          assign tone = 'nudge'
        endif
      elsif measured
        assign msg = 'Shipping from ' | append: cost_money | append: ', or free over ' | append: threshold_money | append: '.'
      else
        assign msg = 'Free shipping on this brand over ' | append: threshold_money | append: '.'
      endif

    elsif measured
      # Everything else — scaling suppliers, shapes with no claim to make. The
      # number is the only honest thing available and it is worth saying.
      assign msg = 'Shipping from ' | append: cost_money | append: '.'
    endif
  -%}

  {%- if msg -%}
    <p class="aj-brand-ship aj-brand-ship--{{ tone }}">
      <span class="aj-brand-ship__text">{{ msg }}</span>
      {%- if tone == 'nudge' -%}
        {%- assign vendor_handle = product.vendor | handle -%}
        <a class="aj-brand-ship__link" href="{{ routes.collections_url }}/vendor-{{ vendor_handle }}">
          Browse {{ product.vendor }}
        </a>
      {%- endif -%}
    </p>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endunless -%}

{% stylesheet %}
  .aj-brand-ship {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  /* A drawn tick rather than an emoji: it inherits colour, scales with the
     text, and does not render as a different glyph on every platform. Only the
     free tone earns it — a tick in front of a line that names a cost reads as
     a benefit that is not being offered. */
  .aj-brand-ship--free::before {
    content: "";
    flex: none;
    width: 0.55rem;
    height: 0.3rem;
    border-left: 2px solid currentColor;
    border-bottom: 2px solid currentColor;
    transform: rotate(-45deg);
    align-self: center;
  }

  /* The pill. A TINT of the brand accent rather than the solid fill the saving
     pill uses, so the two can sit inches apart and still be ranked. Text stays
     the page foreground: at this alpha the surface barely moves, so the
     measured contrast of normal body text carries over — which is not true of
     the solid accent, where #ffde00 is 1.34:1 against white and the text has to
     become ink. */
  .aj-brand-ship--free {
    display: inline-flex;
    width: fit-content;
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
    padding: 0.3rem 0.7rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: color-mix(
      in srgb,
      var(--aj-price-accent, rgb(var(--color-foreground-rgb) / 0.5)) 18%,
      transparent
    );
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  /* Browsers without color-mix get a neutral tint rather than no pill at all —
     the shape is doing most of the work and a transparent background would
     leave the padding looking like a layout bug. */
  @supports not (background: color-mix(in srgb, red 18%, transparent)) {
    .aj-brand-ship--free {
      background: rgb(var(--color-foreground-rgb) / 0.08);
    }
  }

  /* Stated, not sold. */
  .aj-brand-ship--plain {
    color: rgb(var(--color-foreground-rgb) / 0.8);
  }

  .aj-brand-ship--nudge {
    color: rgb(var(--color-foreground-rgb) / 0.8);
  }

  .aj-brand-ship__link {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* Forced colours discard backgrounds, which would flatten the pill into the
     page and lose the only thing separating it from the plain line. */
  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    .aj-brand-ship--free {
      background: Canvas;
      border: 1px solid CanvasText;
    }
  }
{% endstylesheet %}
